106 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 67 0 R
/E (This project presents the design and development of an ESP32-based robotic arm capable of both manual and wireless control. The system integrates Internet of Things \(IoT\) technology to enable real-time communication between the robotic arm and a web-based dashboard using WebSockets. The robotic arm is constructed using four servo motors, which control the base rotation, arm movement, and gripper operation.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
107 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 67 0 R
/E (The ESP32 microcontroller acts as the central unit, processing inputs from both physical joystick modules and the web interface. The dual control system allows users to operate the robotic arm either locally through joysticks or remotely via a browser-based dashboard. The web interface includes features such as virtual joystick control, speed adjustment, and mode selection.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
108 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 67 0 R
/E (In addition to manual control, the system supports automation through manual path programming and playback functionality. Users can define sequences of movements with specific servo angles and speeds, enabling the robotic arm to perform repetitive tasks like pick-and-place operations efficiently.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
109 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 67 0 R
/E (To improve performance and stability, techniques such as dead zone filtering, smooth servo motion, and proper power management are implemented to reduce jitter and ensure reliable operation. The system provides a low-cost, flexible, and scalable solution for robotic automation.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
110 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 67 0 R
/E (This project demonstrates the effective integration of embedded systems, wireless communication, and robotics, making it suitable for applications in industrial automation, education, and research.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
